# CPU Cache Hierarchy & Cache-Friendly Code

## The Cache Hierarchy

Modern CPUs sit between the processor cores and main RAM. Because the CPU is orders of magnitude faster than RAM, a hierarchy of small, fast caches is inserted in between:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                        CPU Cores (GHz)                             │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  L1 Cache   ~32-64 KB per core,  1-4 cycles, split into I/D       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  L2 Cache   ~256 KB - 1 MB per core,  4-12 cycles                 │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  L3 Cache   ~8-64 MB shared,      20-50 cycles                    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Main RAM   ~8-256 GB,             100-300 cycles                 │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Key Concepts

| Concept | Meaning |
|---------|---------|
| **Cache line** | The unit of transfer between cache and memory. Typically **64 bytes**. |
| **Temporal locality** | If you access a value, you'll likely access it again soon. |
| **Spatial locality** | If you access a value, you'll likely access neighbors soon. |
| **Associativity** | How many cache lines can map to the same "set" in the cache (direct-mapped, 2-way, 8-way, fully associative). |
| **Eviction** | When a cache set is full, a replacement policy (usually LRU or pseudo-LRU) kicks out a line. |

When the CPU reads memory, it loads an entire **cache line** (64 bytes), not just the single byte or word requested. This is why spatial locality matters — if you access `arr[0]` then `arr[1]`, the second access is a cache hit because both bytes arrived together.

